  "body": "Gulf News — Property reported a 50 per cent payment threshold. Aldar Properties completed the first registration of a mortgage on an off-plan property in Abu Dhabi, working with Abu Dhabi Commercial Bank under a framework launched by the Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre, according to Aldar.\n\nThe Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre reports that the framework allows buyers who have paid 50 per cent of an eligible off-plan unit's value to mortgage the outstanding amount before construction is complete. Banks can fund remaining instalments during construction and the final payment due at handover, according to the Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre.\n\nAldar states that the 50 per cent payment threshold is aligned with UAE Central Bank regulations. The UAE Central Bank requires customers to have paid 50 per cent of the property price to be eligible for off-plan mortgage financing, according to Aldar.\n\nThe Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre reports that under the system, the financing bank can be recorded on the mortgage registration certificate before handover. The Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre states that mortgage interests in eligible units can be entered in the Initial Real Estate Register, creating a formal record for buyers, developers and lenders.\n\nGhazi Saeed Alateibi, executive director of the real estate transaction sector at the Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre, said the completion of the registration demonstrated the regulator's off-plan mortgage registration service in live market use. Alateibi stated that by enabling mortgage interests in eligible off-plan real estate units to be recorded in the Initial Real Estate Register, the service strengthens transparency and provides greater clarity and protection for buyers, developers and financial institutions.\n\nThe Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre confirmed that the service is available on a market-wide basis to participating institutions that meet the relevant requirements.\n\nThe framework represents a structural shift in Abu Dhabi's off-plan financing architecture. Traditionally, buyers arranged mortgage facilities only at handover—after construction completion and title-deed transfer. That model required purchasers to fund the entire payment schedule through equity or developer-plan instalments, deferring financing certainty until delivery. The new framework allows banks to fund remaining instalments during construction and the final payment due at handover, according to the Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre, enabling buyers to lock borrowing terms and preserve liquidity throughout the build period.\n\nFaisal Falaknaz, Aldar's chief financial and sustainability officer, said the new off-plan mortgage framework introduced by the Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre marks an important step in further enhancing the transparency and accessibility of the market. Falaknaz stated that Aldar is proud to have worked with the Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre and Abu Dhabi Commercial Bank to complete the first transaction, translating the framework into greater financing flexibility for homebuyers.\n\nAldar reports that off-plan financing, including payments during construction, is now available to Aldar customers who have paid 50 per cent or more of the property value at eligible projects. The mortgages are being facilitated without fees through Home Finance by Aldar, an in-house advisory service, according to Aldar.\n\nAldar states that Home Finance by Aldar connects customers to more than six participating conventional and Islamic banks. Participating lenders listed by Aldar include Abu Dhabi Commercial Bank, Abu Dhabi Islamic Bank, Dubai Islamic Bank, Emirates NBD, Emirates Islamic and First Abu Dhabi Bank. Aldar reports that customers can access the service through the Live Aldar app and request a call back from the Home Finance by Aldar team.\n\nThe move could give buyers earlier certainty over borrowing costs and reduce the need to tie up cash while a property is being built, according to Gulf News. Gulf News reports that the framework also gives lenders a formal interest in the property before completion, potentially strengthening protections in the event of a dispute.\n\nThe framework addresses one friction point in off-plan transactions: the gap between a buyer's capital commitment during construction and their ability to secure formal, registered financing against that commitment before handover. For financial institutions, pre-handover mortgage registration creates a documented claim in the Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre's Initial Real Estate Register, according to the regulator, reducing counterparty ambiguity if a developer encounters delivery delays or if a buyer seeks to assign or resell the unit before completion.\n\nThe 50 per cent payment threshold with prudential lending standards. By requiring buyers to self-fund half the purchase price before mortgage eligibility, the framework aligns developer payment plans with regulatory requirements, reducing the risk of over-leveraged buyer cohorts entering the market at early project stages.\n\nFor buyers who have paid 50 per cent of the purchase price, the ability to secure mortgage pre-approval during construction rather than at handover compresses financing risk and may improve capital efficiency, particularly in a rising-rate environment where borrowing costs can shift materially over a construction cycle. The framework gives buyers certainty over financing terms in advance, preserves liquidity during construction, and removes the need to arrange a mortgage at completion, according to Khaleej Times.\n\nKhaleej Times reports that the framework offers an alternative to traditional handover-stage financing with wider choice over rates and terms. The framework does not alter the underlying risk profile of off-plan acquisition. Construction delay, developer solvency, market-price compression during the build period, and resale liquidity at handover remain material considerations. What it does alter is the buyer's ability to formalise financing terms in advance, reducing one source of execution uncertainty in a transaction type that already carries elevated completion and valuation risk relative to secondary-market purchases.\n\nThe Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre has not disclosed eligibility criteria for projects or developers under the framework, nor the volume of registrations processed since the inaugural transaction. For buyers evaluating off-plan opportunities in Abu Dhabi, the new mortgage pathway introduces optionality—but not obligation. Traditional handover-stage financing remains available. The framework's value proposition is strongest for purchasers who prioritise financing certainty over flexibility, who have limited liquidity to fund extended payment plans, or who anticipate interest-rate volatility over the construction horizon and wish to lock terms early.\n\nGulf News reports that the first registration comes as Abu Dhabi's off-plan market expands, with developers increasingly relying on pre-completion sales to fund construction. Gulf News states that the new framework could allow eligible buyers to secure financing earlier, while giving banks a clearer legal position before properties are handed over.",
